.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt LOW-PRIORITY 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm low-priority
.Nd run a command at the lowest priority the system allows
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl v
.Ar command
.Op Ar arg ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs
.Ar command
so that it only gets the CPU, and on Linux the disk, when nothing else
wants them.
Options are read up to the first argument that is not one, so the
command's own are left alone:
.Ql low-priority -v make -j4 .
.Pp
On
.Fx ,
the command runs under
.Ic nice -n 20
in the idle scheduling class,
.Ic idprio 30 .
On Linux, it runs under
.Ic nice -n 19 ,
the
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
policy
.Pq Ic chrt -i 0
and the idle I/O class
.Pq Ic ionice -c 3 .
Elsewhere, under
.Ic nice -n 19
alone.
.Pp
Each scheduling tool but
.Xr nice 1
is first run on
.Xr true 1 ,
and one that is missing or refused is dropped, the command running
under what remains, down to plain
.Xr nice 1 .
Asking the tool is the only test that covers every reason it may
refuse: a sysctl, root, a jail, a container's seccomp filter.
.Nm
then replaces itself with the command.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl v
Say on stderr which tool was skipped, and the command line run, quoted
so that it can be pasted back into a shell.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The exit status of
.Ar command ;
2 for a usage error, a missing
.Ar command
included.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A build that leaves the machine responsive:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ low-priority make -j8
.Ed
.Pp
What it would run, and what it had to drop:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ low-priority -v true
low-priority: running: nice -n 20 idprio 30 true
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chrt 1 ,
.Xr idprio 1 ,
.Xr ionice 1 ,
.Xr nice 1
.Sh CAVEATS
On
.Fx ,
the idle class is had only by root, unless the sysctl
.Va security.bsd.unprivileged_idprio
is 1; otherwise the command runs under plain
.Xr nice 1 .
.Pp
On Linux,
.Ic ionice -c 3
has an effect only with the BFQ or CFQ disk scheduler.
